# Deliberately no [tasks] block, and in particular no `secrets` task.
#
# There used to be one: `mise run secrets` exported the fnox manifest through
# the 1Password CLI into ~/.secrets, guarded against four silent failure modes
# (atomic replace, a same-volume mktemp, a trap, `bash -n`, `--if-missing
# error`). All of that was careful work protecting a file that nothing read.
#
# Measured before removal: ~/.secrets had exactly one writer -- this task -- and
# zero readers. The design named "agents and dotenv-only tools" as its
# consumers, but neither was ever wired. Claude Code has no mechanism that
# sources a dotenv file, and an agent's shell sources ~/.zshrc, which did not
# source ~/.secrets. The only general way to wire it up is to source it at shell
# startup, which makes the key environment-resident -- the thing dotfiles-vv3
# closed against after an environment-resident key leaked twice in one session
# through accidental env dumps.
#
# So it was a plaintext credential sitting on disk, readable by anything running
# as this user, in exchange for nothing. Its one key, LINEAR_API_KEY, reaches
# its one consumer through ~/.config/shell/bd.sh instead: `fnox exec` hands the
# key to a single process and leaves nothing behind.
#
# If a dotenv-only tool ever genuinely needs a file, `fnox export` is one
# command -- write it where that tool reads, not to a machine-wide ~/.secrets.
# See dot_config/fnox/config.toml and
# docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-20-fnox-secrets-design.md.
